description:
An `Inventory` **owns** the `Item`s it holds, as raw pointers. Callers can borrow an item, or take ownership of one out of the inventory:

```cpp
struct Item
{
    static inline int alive{ 0 };
    std::string name;
    int weight;
    Item(std::string n, int w) : name{ std::move(n) }, weight{ w } { ++alive; }
    ~Item() { --alive; }
};

class Inventory
{
public:
    Inventory() = default;
    ~Inventory();
    Inventory(const Inventory&) = delete;
    Inventory& operator=(const Inventory&) = delete;

    Item* add(const std::string& name, int weight);
    void adopt(Item* item);
    Item* find(const std::string& name);
    Item* release(const std::string& name);
    bool destroy(const std::string& name);
    int totalWeight() const;
    int count() const;
};
```

- **add** creates an item with `new` and keeps it; it returns a **borrowed** pointer.
- **adopt** takes ownership of an item the caller made with `new`.
- **find** returns a borrowed pointer to the first item with that name, or `nullptr`.
- **release** removes the first item with that name from the inventory and returns it: the caller now owns it and must delete it. `nullptr` if there is none.
- **destroy** deletes the first item with that name and returns true, or returns false.
- The destructor deletes every item still held. `Item::alive` lets the tests see whether anything leaked or was deleted twice.

